00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.730 align:start position:0%
the UK is unquestionably facing an  unemployment crisis for the latest  figures suggest that for now the wave of  mass redundancies is being held in check  the headline unemployment figure between  March and May was 3.9 percent that's  unchanged from the previous three months  but that's because employment is still  being propped up by the Treasury nine  point four million employees are on the  job furlough Scheme and another 2.7  million self-employed workers have  received payments now many of their jobs  may no longer exist when this support is  withdrawn beginning next month but  there's evidence the labor market is  already crumbling six hundred and forty  nine thousand people left company  payrolls in the three months that June  though they're not all yet formally  considered unemployed and the claimant  count that's those seeking universal  credit or job seekers allowance has  increased by 1.4 million since March  that's up to 2.6 million in total and  the number of vacancies is down by 497  thousand on this time last year that is  a record full the Chancellor of the  Exchequer knows he has a problem the  figures today show that our furlough  scheme has been effective at protecting  millions of people from unemployment but  I understand that people are anxious  which is why last week we outlined our  plan for jobs across the country we will  be able to protect support and create  jobs  these figures don't necessarily account  for the daily toll of a redundancy  announcements that we've heard from some  of the highest profile employers in  British business Sky News has been  tracking these since the outbreak began  and our analysis shows that at least a  thousand job losses have been signaled  every day since lockdown began we can  take it look at some of the worst hit  sectors starting with aviation UK  companies have signaled more than 30,000  job losses with the largest number of  British Airways which is making 12,000  redundancies and what about hospitality  where almost three million workers are  under threat so far more than 12,000  redundancies have been formally signaled  including 5,000 an upper-crust owner SSP  we've also heard peter express his  to close 75 sites placing hundreds more  jobs risk  the collective impact of these losses is  forecast to leave more than three or  perhaps four million people out of work  addressing that will be a defining  challenge of the pandemic
 

